Packet	What Happened
3824	Destination: 333.333.33.33 (my own IP!)
This is a loopback ping - the packet goes out and comes right back!

Key Observations You Should Notice:
1.	TTL (Time To Live) Values
	Your PC → Google:    TTL = 128 (starting value)
	Google → Your PC:    TTL = 111 (decreased by 17 hops!)
Why? Each router the packet passes through subtracts 1 from TTL.
•	Google's reply started with TTL ~128
•	It went through 17 routers to reach you (128 - 111 = 17)
2.	Sequence Numbers: seq=17/4352, seq=18/4608, seq=19/4864
What this means:
•	First number (17, 18, 19) = packet sequence (ping count)
•	Second number (4352, 4608, 4864) = data payload size (growing!)
3.	Response Times (RTT - Round Trip Time)
	33.619 → 33.639 = 20ms   (Google IP)
	39.524 → 39.540 = 16ms   (8.8.8.8)
8.8.8.8 is faster - it's Google's public DNS, likely closer to you!

4. MAC Addresses (From Frame Details)
•	Source MAC: Intel_xx:xx:xx (xx:xx:xx:xx:xx) ← my PC's network card
•	Destination MAC: Calix_xx:xx:xx (xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx) ← my Router
Why? Your PC sends the packet to your router first (MAC address), then the router forwards it to Google.
